"If the dose of a drug is 17.5 mg/m²/day, how many milligrams of the drug should be administered daily to a patient weighing 65 lb and measuring 3 ft 6 in. in height?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
If the dose of a drug is 17.5 mg/m²/day, how many milligrams of the drug should be administered daily to a patient weighing 65 lb and measuring 3 ft 6 in. in height?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the dose of the drug, we first need to determine the patient's surface-sectional area (SA) in m². The formula for surface-sectional area is:

\[
\text{SA} = \sqrt{\frac{14 \times \text{mass (kg)} \times \text{height (cm)}}{10^2}}
\]

First, convert the patient's weight from pounds to kilograms:

\[
65 \text{ lb} \times \frac{1 \text{ kg}}{2.22 \text{ lb}} = 31.6 \text{ kg}
\]

Next, convert the patient's height from feet and inches to centimeters:

\[
3 \text{ ft} \times 25.4 \text{ cm/ft} + 6 \text{ in} \times 2.54 \text{ cm/in} = 93.2 \text{ cm}
\]

Now, calculate the surface-sectional area:

\[
\text{SA} = \sqrt{\frac{14 \times 31.6 \times 93.2}{10^2}} = 1.24 \text{ m}^2
\]

Finally, calculate the dose of the drug:

\[
\text{Dose} = 17.5 \text{ mg/m}^2 \times 1.24 \text{ m}^2 = 20.5 \text{ mg}
\]

So, the dose of the drug should be \(\boxed{20.5 \text{ mg}}\) administered daily to the patient.
