The presence of cancer in axillary lymph nodes (also known as adjuvant breast cancer) is called adjuvant lymph node therapy. This is important because it indicates that the cancer may spread to other parts of the body, and it can help predict the likelihood of the cancer spreading again.

Axillary lymph node status is determined by a test called adjuvant lymph node biopsy, which involves collecting a small sample of lymph nodes from the ovaries. The biopsy is then tested to determine if cancer cells are present.

The status of axillary lymph nodes can help guide the following aspects of breast cancer treatment:

1. **Treatment type and extent**: Patients with adjuvant lymph node therapy may need more aggressive treatment, such as hormone therapy, chemotherapy, or targeted therapy, to prevent the cancer from spreading. Patients with no adjuvant lymph node therapy may not need these treatments, or may only need hormone therapy.

