","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = Σn * ΔS_f(products) - Σn * ΔS_f(reactants)

where ΔS_f is the standard entropy of formation for each substance, and n is the number of moles.

For the reaction:

3 CO(g) + 3 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

The standard entropies of formation (S_f) at 298 K are:

S_f(CO) = 188.7 J/mol·K
S_f(O2) = 205.1 J/mol·K
S_f(CO2) = 213.6 J/mol·K

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [3 * S_f(CO2)] - [3 * S_f(CO) + 3 * S_f(O2)]

ΔS = [3 * 213.6 J/mol·K] - [3 * 188.7 J/mol·K + 3 * 205.1 J/mol·K]

