moles of compound in standard solution = \( C_s \times 0.01 V \)

where \( C_s \) is the concentration of the standard solution.

Now, we can set up the mass balance equation:

0.00125 moles = moles of compound in water sample + \( C_s \times 0.01 V \)

To solve for the moles of compound in the water sample, we need the concentration of the standard solution (\( C_s \)) and the volume of the standard solution added (\( V \)). Once we have this information, we can calculate the moles of compound in the water sample and then find the concentration of the compound in the original water sample:

concentration of compound in water sample = moles of compound in water sample / volume of water sample
