What is the relationship used to estimate the flammability limits of mixtures from the data for individual fuels?,"<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the relationship used to estimate the flammability limits of mixtures from the data for individual fuels?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The relationship used to estimate the flammability limits of mixtures from the data for individual fuels is the **Leiblich equation**. It is a semi-empirical equation that relates the flammability limits of a mixture to the mole fractions of the components. The equation is given by:

\[
\frac{1}{L_i} = \sum_{i=1}^{i=i} \frac{y_i}{L_i^0}
\]

where:
- \(L_i\) is the lower flammability limit (LFL) of the mixture,
- \(y_i\) is the mole fraction of component \(i\) in the mixture,
- \(L_i^0\) is the LFL of pure component \(i\),
- \(i\) is the number of components in the mixture.

This equation assumes that the flammability limits of a mixture are inversely proportional to the mole fractions of the components. This means that the LFL of a mixture will be lower than the LFL of the most flammable component, and the UFL (upper flammability limit) will be higher than the UFL of the least flammable component.
