","The reaction between solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) is a neutralization reaction. The observable effects of this reaction are:
1. **Formation of a white precipitate**: The reaction produces sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)) and water (H₂O). Sodium benzoate is a salt that is soluble in water, but it forms a white precipitate when the solution is saturated.
2. **Cooling of the solution**: The reaction is exothermic, meaning it releases heat. This can be observed as a cooling of the solution.
3. **No gas evolution**: Unlike some other neutralization reactions, this reaction does not produce any gaseous products.

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:
\[
\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(s) + \text{NaOH(aq)} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)}
\]

**Explanation**:
- The reaction involves the transfer of a proton (H⁺) from benzoic acid to sodium hydroxide.
- The proton from benzoic acid combines with the hydroxide ion (OH⁻) from sodium hydroxide to form water (H₂O).
- The remaining benzoate ion (C₇H₅O₂⁻) combines with the sodium ion (Na⁺) to form sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)).
- The reaction is balanced, with one mole of benzoic acid reacting with one mole of sodium hydroxide to produce one mole of sodium benzoate and one mole of water.

\[
\boxed{\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(s) + \text{OH}^-(aq) \rightarrow \text{C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2^-(aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)}}
\]
