","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: structural isomerism and optical isomerism.

1. Structural isomerism: This occurs when the molecules have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms in their molecular structure. In C4H10O, there are two types of structural isomerism:
   a. Chain isomerism: This occurs when the carbon chain is different. For example, butane (C4H10) can exist as n-butane (straight chain) or 2-methylpropane (branched chain).
   b. Functional group isomerism: This occurs when the same molecular formula has different functional groups. For example, C4H10O can be either ethanol (CH3CH2CH2OH) or diethyl ether (CH3CH2OCH2CH3).

2. Optical isomerism: This occurs when a molecule has a chiral center, which is a carbon atom bonded to four different groups. When a molecule has a chiral center, it can exist in two non-superimposable mirror images, called enantiomers. In C4H10O, the molecule 2-butanol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3) has a chiral center at the second carbon atom, and thus exhibits optical isomerism.

In summary, the possible types of isomerism in a molecule with the chemical formula C4H10O are:
- Chain isomerism (structural isomerism)
- Functional group isomerism (structural isomerism)
-Optical isomerism (stereochemical isomerism)
