","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a type of asymmetric synthesis that involves the formation of a single enantiomer (or stereoisomer) of a product with 100% enantiomeric excess (ee). This means that the reaction produces only one stereoisomer, with no contamination from the other enantiomer. This is the most level of enantioselectivity possible in organic synthesis.  

The key difference between asymmetric asymmetric synthesis and other types of asymmetric synthesis is the level of enantioselectivity. Other types of asymmetric synthesis, such as enantioselective synthesis, can produce a high ee of one enantiomer (e.g., 90% ee), but they still produce a small amount of the other enantiomer.  

For example, in enantioselective synthesis, the reaction may preferentially form one enantiomer over the other, but the reaction mixture may still contain a small amount of the other enantiomer. In contrast, in absolute asymmetric synthesis, the reaction produces only one enantiomer, with no contamination from the other enantiomer.  

An example of asymmetric synthesis is the synthesis of the amino acid phenylalanine. The amino acid phenylalanine has a chiral center at the alpha carbon, and it exists as two enantiomers: L-phenylalanine and D-phenylalanine. In asymmetric synthesis, chemists can design reactions that preferentially form one enantiomer over the other. For example, the Sharmann reaction can be used to synthesize L-phenylalanine with high enantioselectivity.  

An example of absolute asymmetric synthesis would be a reaction that produces only L-phenylalanine, with no D-phenylalanine formed. This would require a catalyst or reagent that can selectively attack one enantiomer of the starting material, and not the other.  

In summary, asymmetric asymmetric synthesis is a more precise and more efficient way to synthesize chiral compounds, as it allows for the production of enantiomers with 100% enantiomeric excess.
