","The terms ""isotoplogues"" and ""isotopomers"" are used to describe molecules with different isotopic compositions. However, they refer to different types of isotopic substitutions:  
1. **Isotoplogues**: Isotoplogues are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ only in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) can be \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \), \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2^{13}\text{O}_3 \), or \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3^{18}\text{O} \).  
2. **Isotopomers**: Isotopomers are molecules that have the same molecular formula and the same connectivity of atoms but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms.  
   - For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) and \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2^{13}\text{O}_3 \) are isotopomers because the carbon and oxygen atoms are bonded in the same order, but the \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2^{13}\text{O}_3 \) molecule has one \( ^{13}\text{C} \) atom instead of a \( ^1\text{C} \) atom.  
   - However, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) and \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3^{18}\text{O} \) are not isotopomers because the oxygen atoms are bonded differently.  

Thus, **isotoplogues are a broader term** that include isotopomers, but isotopomers are more specific and refer to molecules with the same connectivity of atoms.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isotoplogues: Same molecular formula, different isotopic composition.} \\
&\text{Isotopomers: Same molecular formula, same connectivity, different isotopic composition.}
\end{aligned}
}
