","The Fourier transform of a function \( f(t) \) is given by:

\[
F(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(t) e^{-i\omega t} \, dt
\]

For the given function \( f(t) = 3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t) \), the Fourier transform is:

\[
F(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \left( 3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t) \right) e^{-i\omega t} \, dt
\]

To evaluate this integral, we can separate it into two separate integrals:

\[
F(\omega) = 3 \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-i\omega t} \, dt + 2 \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-i\omega t} \, dt
\]

The Fourier transform of \( \sin(2\pi t) \) is:

\[
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-i\omega t} \, dt = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{2\pi - \omega} - \frac{1}{2\pi + \omega} \right)
\]

The Fourier transform of \( \cos(4\pi t) \) is:

\[
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-i\omega t} \, dt = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{4\pi - \omega} + \frac{1}{4\pi + \omega} \right)
\]

Substituting these results back into the Fourier transform of \( f(t) \):

\[
F(\omega) = 3 \cdot \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{2\pi - \omega} - \frac{1}{2\pi + \omega} \right) + 2 \cdot \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{4\pi - \omega} + \frac{1}{4\pi + \omega} \right)
\]

Simplifying:

\[
F(\omega) = \frac{3}{2} \left( \frac{1}{2\pi - \omega} - \frac{1}{2\pi + \omega} \right) + \left( \frac{1}{4\pi - \omega} + \frac{1}{4\pi + \omega} \right)
\]

Thus, the Fourier transform of \( f(t) = 3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t) \) is:

\[
F(\omega) = \frac{3}{2} \left( \frac{1}{2\pi - \omega} - \frac{1}{2\pi + \omega} \right) + \left( \frac{1}{4\pi - \omega} + \frac{1}{4\pi + \omega} \right)
\]
