","To test the differentiability at \( x = 0 \), we analyze the limit of the derivative at \( x = 0 \):
\[
\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{f(x) - f(0)}{x - 0} = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{x \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)}{x} = \lim_{x \to 0} \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{x}\right}.
\]
As \( x \to 0 \), \( \frac{1}{x} \to \pm \infty \), so \( \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) \to \frac{\pi}{2} \) (or \( -\frac{\pi}{2} \), depending on the sign of \( x \)). Since the limit does not exist, the function is not differentiable at \( x = 0 \). 

**Final Answer:**
The function is not differentiable at \( x = 0 \).
