","a) **Retrospective** - The study is looking at the smoking habits of people who already have a specific condition (bladder cancer).  
b) **Cross-sectional** - The study records the eating habits and general health of the nurses at a single point of time.  
c) **Prospective** - The study observes the eating and exercise patterns of the plants over time to predict the likelihood of cancer later up the future.  
d) **Cross-sectional** - The study compares the prices of the two types of items at the same time point.  

**Explanation:**  
- **Retrospective** studies examine the relationship between a variable (e.g., smoking) and an outcome (e.g., bladder cancer) by analyzing information about the variable and outcome that already occurred.  
- **Cross-sectional** studies measure the presence of variables (e.g., smoking habits, general health) and outcomes (e.g., cancer) in a group of time at a single point of time.  
- **Prospective** studies observe a group of time over time to determine if a certain variable (e.g., eating and exercise patterns) is associated with an outcome (e.g., cancer).
