     - \( b \notin H \) (since \( y b < 0 \)).
     - \( b \) lies in the half-space \( y^T x < 0 \).

Thus, we have shown that for any point \( b \notin C \), there exists a linear hyperplane \( H \) (through \( 0 \)) that divides \( C \) and \( b \) into two half-spaces.

