## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The proton accelerates due to the electric field, which exerts a force on it. The force \(F\) on the proton is given by:  
\[ F = qE \]  
where \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) is the charge of the proton and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\) is the electric field strength. Substituting the values:  
\[ F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19}) \times (500) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N} \]  
The acceleration \(a\) of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} \]  
where \(m = 1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) is the mass of the proton. Substituting the values:  
\[ a = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27}} = 4.78 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.78 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The proton starts from rest (\(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\)) and accelerates to a speed of \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\). The time \(t\) taken to achieve this speed is given by the kinematic equation:  
\[ v = v_0 + at \]  
Solving for \(t\):  
\[ t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 - 0}{4.78 \times 10^{10}} = 6.30 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{s} \]  
Thus, the time taken is \(\boxed{6.30 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{s}}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

