   \[
   \text{Flow velocity} = \frac{\text{Flow rate}}{\text{Cross-sectional area}}
   \]
   The cross-sectional area of the tubing is:
   \[
   \text{Area} = \pi \left(\frac{0.1 \text{ mm}}{2}\right)^2 = \pi \left(\frac{0.1 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}}{2}\right)^2 = 3.14 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m}^2
   \]
   The flow velocity is:
   \[
   \text{Flow velocity} = \frac{10 \text{ cm}^3 \text{ min}^{-1}}{3.14 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m}^2} = \frac{10 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}^3 \text{ min}^{-1}}{3.14 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m}^2} = 3.17 \times 10^2 \text{ m/min}
   \]
   Convert to m/s:
   \[
   \text{Flow velocity} = 3.17 \times 10^2 \text{ m/min} \times \frac{1 \text{ min}}{60 \text{ s}} = 5.28 \text{ m/s}
   \]

2. **Calculate the residence time for different flow tube lengths:**
   \[
   \text{Residence time} = \frac{\text{Tube length}}{\text{Flow velocity}}
   \]
