<USER_REQUEST>
A polycrystalline aluminium alloy contains hard particles with a diameter of \(10^{-8}\) m and an average center-to-center spacing of \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m in the slip planes. The shear modulus \(G\) of aluminium is 26 GN m\(^{-2}\), and the Burgers vector \(b\) is 0.286 nm. Estimate the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The contribution of hard particles to the tensile yield strength of an alloy is given by the Oertey-type equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + \frac{G b^2}{d}
\]  
where:  
- \(\sigma_{yt}\) is the tensile yield strength of the pure alloy (without particles),  
- \(G\) is the shear modulus,  
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector,  
- \(d\) is the center-to-center spacing of the particles.  

Given:  
- \(d = 6 \times 10^{-8}\) m  
- \(G = 26 \times 10^9\) N m\(^{-2}\)  
- \(b = 0.286 \times 10^{-9}\) m  

Substituting these values:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + \frac{(26 \times 10^9) \times (0.286 \times 10^{-9})^2}{6 \times 10^{-8}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + 2.4 \times 10^3 \text{ N m}^{-2}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of the particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{2.4 \times 10^3 \text{ N m}^{-2}}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

