A concave spherical mirror has a radius of curvature of 25 cm. A point source of light is placed at a distance of 10 cm from the mirror's vertex along its principal axis. If the mirror has a diameter of 20 cm and a center thickness of 2 cm, what is the spherical aberration of the mirror?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the spherical aberration of the mirror, we first need to determine the focal length \( f \) and the radius of curvature \( R \). The focal length is half the radius of curvature:  
\[
f = \frac{R}{2} = \frac{25 \text{ cm}}{2} = 12.5 \text{ cm}
\]  
The spherical aberration \( \delta \) is given by:  
\[
\delta = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{a^2}{R} - \frac{a^2}{f} \right)
\]  
where \( a \) is the radius of the mirror (half the diameter), which is \( 10 \text{ cm} \). Substituting the values:  
\[
\delta = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{(10 \text{ cm})^2}{25 \text{ cm}} - \frac{(10 \text{ cm})^2}{12.5 \text{ cm}} \right) = \frac{1}{2} \left( 4 \text{ cm} - 8 \text{ cm} \right) = -2 \text{ cm}
\]  
The negative sign indicates that the spherical aberration is of the second order, which means the light rays are focused slightly behind the principal focus.  

Thus, the spherical aberration is \(\boxed{-2 \text{ cm}}\).
