","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton scattering formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2\pi}{m_e c} \left( 1 - \cos \theta \right)
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,  
- \(m_e\) is the mass of the electron (\(9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),  
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),  
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.  

The maximum change in wavelength occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (i.e., the electron is scattered head-to-forward). Substituting \(\theta = 180^\circ\) into the formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2\pi}{m_e c}
\]  
The percentage change is:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda_0} \times 100
\]  
Substituting the given wavelength \(\lambda_0 = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}\):  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \frac{\frac{2\pi}{m_e c}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100
\]  
Calculating the above expression gives:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} \approx 0.00025 \, \text{or} \, 0.025\%
\]  
Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the photon is \(\boxed{0.025\%}\).
